{"module":"women","voice":"female","duration":153.3,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"The women who organized the movement","start":0.0,"end":2.35,"words":[[0.06,0.22],[0.22,0.62],[0.62,0.78],[0.78,1.42],[1.42,1.58],[1.58,2.15]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Black women did much of the organizing behind the movement's best-known moments, often without the microphone.","start":3.15,"end":9.82,"words":[[3.21,3.48],[3.48,3.82],[3.82,4.03],[4.03,4.31],[4.31,4.44],[4.44,4.58],[4.58,5.2],[5.2,5.68],[5.68,5.82],[5.82,6.37],[6.37,6.92],[6.92,7.54],[7.78,8.06],[8.06,8.47],[8.47,8.61],[8.61,9.37]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Founders","start":10.17,"end":11.15,"words":[[10.25,11.0]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Ida B. Wells investigated lynchings at the risk of her life.","start":11.85,"end":15.97,"words":[[11.9,12.16],[12.16,12.36],[12.69,12.95],[12.95,13.81],[13.81,14.34],[14.34,14.47],[14.47,14.6],[14.6,14.86],[14.86,15.0],[15.0,15.13],[15.13,15.46]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Mary Church Terrell co-founded the National Association of Colored Women (1896) and, at 89, won the lawsuit that desegregated Washington, D.C.'s restaurants (District of Columbia v. John R.","start":16.32,"end":30.99,"words":[[16.36,16.74],[16.74,17.13],[17.13,17.52],[17.52,18.29],[18.29,18.44],[18.44,18.98],[18.98,19.91],[19.91,20.07],[20.07,20.45],[20.45,20.84],[20.84,22.0],[22.27,22.58],[22.85,23.0],[23.0,23.7],[23.97,24.2],[24.2,24.36],[24.36,24.74],[24.74,24.97],[24.97,25.98],[25.98,26.67],[26.94,27.33],[27.33,28.03],[28.03,28.64],[28.64,28.8],[28.8,29.42],[29.42,29.65],[30.03,30.34],[30.34,30.5]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Thompson Co., 1953).","start":31.34,"end":33.7,"words":[[31.39,31.81],[31.81,32.04],[32.25,33.2]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Montgomery's women","start":34.6,"end":36.27,"words":[[34.65,35.66],[35.66,36.12]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In 1944 Rosa Parks investigated the rape of Recy Taylor for the NAACP, years before her bus protest.","start":36.97,"end":45.01,"words":[[37.02,37.17],[37.17,38.15],[38.15,38.53],[38.53,38.83],[38.83,39.81],[39.81,39.97],[39.97,40.27],[40.27,40.42],[40.42,40.8],[40.8,41.17],[41.17,41.32],[41.32,41.47],[41.47,42.53],[42.8,43.1],[43.1,43.4],[43.4,43.55],[43.55,43.78],[43.78,44.46]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Fifteen-year-old Claudette Colvin refused to give up her bus seat in March 1955, nine months before Parks.","start":45.36,"end":53.11,"words":[[45.37,46.46],[46.46,46.93],[46.93,47.4],[47.4,47.95],[47.95,48.11],[48.11,48.34],[48.34,48.5],[48.5,48.66],[48.66,48.89],[48.89,49.13],[49.13,49.28],[49.28,49.6],[49.6,50.93],[51.21,51.52],[51.52,51.91],[51.91,52.22],[52.22,52.62]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Colvin and Aurelia Browder were among the plaintiffs in Browder v. Gayle, the case that ended Montgomery's bus segregation.","start":53.46,"end":62.55,"words":[[53.46,53.95],[53.95,54.2],[54.2,54.77],[54.77,55.27],[55.27,55.43],[55.43,55.76],[55.76,55.92],[55.92,56.66],[56.66,56.82],[56.82,57.32],[57.32,57.56],[57.97,58.38],[58.67,58.83],[58.83,59.16],[59.16,59.41],[59.41,59.82],[59.82,60.72],[60.72,60.97],[60.97,61.95]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Jo Ann Robinson's Women's Political Council launched the boycott.","start":62.9,"end":66.74,"words":[[62.96,63.22],[63.22,63.35],[63.35,63.93],[63.93,64.31],[64.31,64.89],[64.89,65.34],[65.34,65.73],[65.73,65.86],[65.86,66.31]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Respectability: a strategy debated","start":67.64,"end":70.31,"words":[[67.69,68.69],[68.92,69.06],[69.06,69.66],[69.66,70.19]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Leaders chose Rosa Parks rather than Claudette Colvin as the face of the boycott, partly because Colvin was a pregnant teenager.","start":71.01,"end":78.68,"words":[[71.06,71.43],[71.43,71.79],[71.79,72.15],[72.15,72.44],[72.44,72.73],[72.73,72.95],[72.95,73.38],[73.38,73.82],[73.82,73.96],[73.96,74.11],[74.11,74.4],[74.4,74.54],[74.54,74.69],[74.69,75.2],[75.45,75.81],[75.81,76.18],[76.18,76.61],[76.61,76.83],[76.83,76.97],[76.97,77.55],[77.55,78.21]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Historians and activists still debate “respectability politics”: whether presenting the most sympathetic plaintiffs helped win public support, or excluded people and narrowed whose dignity counted.","start":79.03,"end":91.25,"words":[[79.08,79.8],[79.8,80.01],[80.01,80.66],[80.66,80.95],[80.95,81.38],[81.38,82.39],[82.39,83.03],[83.28,83.57],[83.57,84.22],[84.22,84.36],[84.36,84.72],[84.72,85.44],[85.44,86.09],[86.09,86.45],[86.45,86.66],[86.66,87.1],[87.1,87.53],[87.78,87.85],[87.85,88.5],[88.5,88.86],[88.86,89.07],[89.07,89.51],[89.51,89.72],[89.72,90.22],[90.22,90.8]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Organizers and strategists","start":92.15,"end":94.09,"words":[[92.2,92.88],[92.88,93.11],[93.11,94.02]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Ella Baker built the NAACP's branches, ran the SCLC's first office and guided SNCC.","start":94.79,"end":101.53,"words":[[94.83,95.07],[95.07,95.45],[95.45,95.77],[95.77,95.92],[95.92,96.94],[96.94,97.64],[97.91,98.14],[98.14,98.3],[98.3,99.0],[99.0,99.31],[99.31,99.62],[99.62,99.86],[99.86,100.33],[100.33,101.03]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Septima Clark's Citizenship Schools taught thousands of adults to read and pass voter tests.","start":101.88,"end":107.99,"words":[[101.92,102.48],[102.48,102.89],[102.89,103.7],[103.7,104.1],[104.1,104.35],[104.35,105.0],[105.0,105.16],[105.16,105.64],[105.64,105.81],[105.81,106.05],[106.05,106.29],[106.29,106.54],[106.54,106.94],[106.94,107.43]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Diane Nash led the Nashville sit-ins and kept the Freedom Rides going in 1961.","start":108.34,"end":113.65,"words":[[108.39,108.8],[108.8,109.01],[109.01,109.21],[109.21,109.35],[109.35,109.76],[109.76,110.17],[110.17,110.38],[110.38,110.65],[110.65,110.79],[110.79,111.2],[111.2,111.54],[111.54,111.88],[111.88,112.02],[112.02,113.18]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Fannie Lou Hamer was beaten in a Winona, Mississippi, jail in 1963 for organizing voters.","start":114.0,"end":121.3,"words":[[114.01,114.32],[114.32,114.47],[114.47,114.85],[114.85,115.08],[115.08,115.46],[115.46,115.62],[115.62,115.77],[115.77,116.38],[116.65,117.34],[117.6,117.99],[117.99,118.14],[118.14,119.36],[119.36,119.52],[119.52,120.21],[120.21,120.74]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Dorothy Height helped plan the March on Washington, where almost no women were allowed to speak.","start":121.65,"end":127.2,"words":[[121.7,122.13],[122.13,122.41],[122.41,122.76],[122.76,123.04],[123.04,123.18],[123.18,123.47],[123.47,123.61],[123.61,124.24],[124.49,124.7],[124.7,125.19],[125.19,125.41],[125.41,125.76],[125.76,125.9],[125.9,126.25],[126.25,126.39],[126.39,126.75]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Today","start":128.1,"end":128.88,"words":[[128.15,128.71]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Alicia Garza, Patrisse Cullors and Opal Tometi started Black Lives Matter in 2013.","start":129.58,"end":136.03,"words":[[129.63,130.04],[130.04,130.45],[130.74,131.23],[131.23,131.64],[131.64,131.89],[131.89,132.3],[132.3,132.79],[132.79,133.29],[133.29,133.61],[133.61,134.03],[134.03,134.35],[134.35,134.52],[134.52,135.5]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The #SayHerName campaign (2014) brought attention to Black women killed by police, such as Breonna Taylor in 2020.","start":136.38,"end":144.96,"words":[[136.41,136.56],[136.56,137.46],[137.46,137.99],[137.99,138.81],[139.08,139.38],[139.38,139.9],[139.9,140.05],[140.05,140.35],[140.35,140.73],[140.73,141.03],[141.03,141.25],[141.25,141.7],[141.97,142.27],[142.27,142.42],[142.42,142.94],[142.94,143.32],[143.32,143.47],[143.47,144.44]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Black women are among the most reliable voters in America, and their organizing has decided close elections.","start":145.31,"end":152.36,"words":[[145.36,145.65],[145.65,146.02],[146.02,146.09],[146.09,146.38],[146.38,146.52],[146.52,146.89],[146.89,147.54],[147.54,147.97],[147.97,148.12],[148.12,148.7],[148.95,149.17],[149.17,149.38],[149.38,150.04],[150.04,150.25],[150.25,150.83],[150.83,151.19],[151.19,151.85]]}]}